Definition 4.3 (Sampling Distribution) The sampling distribution of an estimator \(\hat\theta=\hat{\theta}(X_1,\ldots,X_n)\) is the probability distribution of \(\hat\theta\) induced by the joint distribution of \((X_1,\ldots,X_n)\).




The properties 4.2 of an estimator, such as bias and standard error, are defined via this distribution.
In general, however, deriving an exact sampling distribution is hard, because:

It depends on the chosen statistical model for the data (the distribution of \(X_i\)).

The estimator \(\hat\theta\) may be a complicated/nonlinear function of the sample.

In a few special cases, however, we can derive the full sampling distribution of the estimator.







Example 4.1 (Normal Random Sample: Sampling Distribution of \(\bar{X}\)) If \[
X_1,\dots,X_n \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(\mu,\sigma^2),
\] then \[
\bar{X} \;\sim\; \mathcal{N}\!\left(\mu,\ \frac{\sigma^2}{n}\right).
\]










TipSolution






Solution 4.2. Averages are linear combinations: \(\bar X=\tfrac1n\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\). By the properties of the normal distribution 2.4.1, a linear combination of independent Normal variables is Normal. Moreover, \[
\mathrm{E}[\bar X]=\tfrac1n\sum \mathrm{E}[X_i]=\mu,\qquad
\mathrm{Var}(\bar X)=\tfrac{1}{n^2}\sum \mathrm{Var}(X_i)=\frac{\sigma^2}{n}.
\] So \(\bar X\sim \mathcal N\!\left(\mu,\sigma^2/n\right)\).











Example 4.2 (Normal Random Sample: Sampling Distribution of \(S^2\)) If \[
X_1,\dots,X_n \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(\mu,\sigma^2),
\] then \[
S^2 \;\sim\; \chi^2_{n-1}
\]










TipSolution






Solution 4.2. Averages are linear combinations: \(\bar X=\tfrac1n\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\). By the properties of the normal distribution 2.4.1, a linear combination of independent Normal variables is Normal. Moreover, \[
\mathrm{E}[\bar X]=\tfrac1n\sum \mathrm{E}[X_i]=\mu,\qquad
\mathrm{Var}(\bar X)=\tfrac{1}{n^2}\sum \mathrm{Var}(X_i)=\frac{\sigma^2}{n}.
\] So \(\bar X\sim \mathcal N\!\left(\mu,\sigma^2/n\right)\).

5.2 Likelihood Function
So far we have described the likelihood informally as “the joint density of the data given the parameter.”
We now give a formal definition and introduce the notation we will use in the frequentist parts of the course.







Definition 5.1 Likelihood Function. For fixed observed data \(\underline{x}\), the likelihood function is defined as
\[
L(\theta ; \underline{x}) \coloneqq f(\,\underline{x} \mid \theta).
\]
Here we treat \(\theta\) as the variable, and the data \(\underline{x}\) as fixed.










TipRemark (Likelihood vs. Probability)






Remark 5.2 (Likelihood vs. Probability). It is important to distinguish between the joint density of the data and the likelihood function:

Joint density (PDF/PMF). \(f(\underline{x}\mid \theta)\) is viewed as a function of the data \(\underline{x}\), with the parameter \(\theta\) fixed. This describes the probability distribution of the sample under \(\theta\).
Likelihood function. The same expression, written as
\[
L(\theta;\underline{x}) = f(\underline{x}\mid\theta),
\]
is instead viewed as a function of the parameter \(\theta\), with the observed data \(\underline{x}\) held fixed. This is the function we use to make inferences about \(\theta\).


In short:

Joint density: given \(\theta\), how likely are different data values \(\underline{x}\)?
Likelihood: given observed \(\underline{x}\), how plausible are different values of \(\theta\)?






